---
import CaseStudyLayout from '../../layouts/en/CaseStudyLayout.astro';

const metrics = [
  { icon: '💰', value: '€96k', label: 'Revenue at Risk Identified' },
  { icon: '🎯', value: '€42k', label: 'Cross-Sell Opportunity' },
  { icon: '📊', value: '100k+', label: 'Transactions Analyzed' },
  { icon: '📈', value: '5', label: 'Customer Segments' }
];
---

<CaseStudyLayout
  title="E-Commerce Analytics Dashboard"
  description="RFM segmentation and cohort analysis revealing hidden churn patterns and cross-sell opportunities in transactional data."
  projectType="Business Intelligence"
  stack={['Power BI', 'SQL', 'DAX', 'RFM Analysis']}
  metrics={metrics}
  githubUrl="https://github.com/jose-galvao13/ecommerce-analytics"
  currentLang="en"
>
  <section>
    <h2>🎯 Why I Built This</h2>
    <p>
      This is a self-directed project on a public Brazilian e-commerce transaction dataset (~100k orders), built to practice a common pattern: aggregate revenue can look healthy while hiding churn among high-value customers, because a flat top-line number doesn't show who's actually still buying.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Aggregate Blindness:</strong> total revenue trends can mask churn happening within specific customer segments.</li>
      <li><strong>No Segmentation:</strong> treating every customer the same hides where marketing spend would be best targeted.</li>
      <li><strong>Missed Cross-Sell:</strong> without looking at product affinity, it's easy to miss which products are commonly bought together.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>💡 Solution: RFM Analysis & Cohort Tracking</h2>
    <p>
      Built <strong>Power BI dashboard</strong> using RFM (Recency, Frequency, Monetary) segmentation to identify at-risk customers and cross-sell opportunities.
    </p>
    
    <h3>RFM Segmentation Model</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Recency:</strong> Days since last purchase (1=recent, 5=dormant).</li>
      <li><strong>Frequency:</strong> # orders in last 12 months (1=one-time, 5=loyal).</li>
      <li><strong>Monetary:</strong> Total spend (1=low-value, 5=VIP).</li>
      <li><strong>Segments:</strong> Champions (555), At-Risk (511), Lost (155), etc.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Implementation</h3>
    <pre><code>-- SQL: Calculate RFM Scores
WITH rfm_base AS (
  SELECT 
    customer_id,
    DATEDIFF(day, MAX(order_date), GETDATE()) as recency,
    COUNT(order_id) as frequency,
    SUM(order_value) as monetary
  FROM orders
  WHERE order_date >= DATEADD(year, -1, GETDATE())
  GROUP BY customer_id
)
SELECT 
  customer_id,
  NTILE(5) OVER (ORDER BY recency DESC) as R_score,
  NTILE(5) OVER (ORDER BY frequency ASC) as F_score,
  NTILE(5) OVER (ORDER BY monetary ASC) as M_score,
  CONCAT(R_score, F_score, M_score) as RFM_segment
FROM rfm_base</code></pre>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>📈 What the Data Shows</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>~€96k in Revenue at Risk:</strong> a segment of previously high-value ("Champion") customers who hadn't purchased in 60+ days — this is the revenue tied to those customers' historical spend, not a guaranteed loss.</li>
      <li><strong>~€42k Cross-Sell Signal:</strong> customer groups who buy one product category but not a commonly-paired one, flagged as a candidate for a cross-sell campaign.</li>
      <li><strong>Cohort Retention:</strong> month-1 retention in the cohort analysis came out low, which would be worth investigating as a possible onboarding issue in a real business.</li>
    </ul>
    <p><em>These are patterns surfaced in the dataset, not measured outcomes from an actual retention or marketing campaign — no campaign was run against this data.</em></p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>🛠️ Dashboard Features</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>RFM Heatmap:</strong> Visual grid showing customer distribution across segments.</li>
      <li><strong>Cohort Tables:</strong> Month-over-month retention curves by acquisition cohort.</li>
      <li><strong>Product Affinity Matrix:</strong> Which products are frequently bought together?</li>
      <li><strong>Customer Lifetime Value:</strong> Predicted LTV based on RFM segment + purchase history.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>🚀 Future Enhancements</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Predictive Churn Model:</strong> ML model predicting 30-day churn probability.</li>
      <li><strong>Automated Campaigns:</strong> Trigger emails to "At-Risk" customers automatically.</li>
      <li><strong>Real-Time RFM:</strong> Update scores daily (currently weekly batch).</li>
    </ul>
  </section>
</CaseStudyLayout>
